// Guards the seam `stepSelfhost` sits on (#3152). `stepSelfhost`
// (`artifactsteps.bit`) links THIS TREE's `libbitrt.a` (built from current
// `runtime/**` source) against a compiler produced by compiling
// `compiler/**` with the PINNED stage0 (the `stage0 build compiler` line
// further down in the same function) — a previous release that never saw
// the current source.
//
// Both compilers are internally correct for the ABI each was written against.
// The break is that `Op.RtCall` lowering (the compiler backend's lowering for
// slice/map/chan-literal syntax, e.g. `[]T(n)`) hardcodes its callee's
// argument count into whichever compiler emits the call — a property of that
// compiler's own already-compiled machine code, not something `stage0 build
// compiler` re-derives from `runtime/**` source at build time. So when a
// `runtime/**` change adds a trailing parameter to an exported `bit_rt_*`
// symbol, stage0 keeps emitting calls at the OLD arity no matter what the
// tree's own copy of that function now declares. The linker resolves a
// symbol NAME, not a signature, so this links cleanly; the callee then reads
// an argument register the caller never wrote. #3152's concrete case:
// `runtime/root/slices.bit`'s `rtSliceNew` gained a trailing `elemSize`
// parameter, and the resulting `bit-out/bin/bit` allocated 11.3 GB in 3
// seconds compiling a 12-line program — `elemSize` arriving as whatever `x3`
// happened to hold, multiplied into `gcAllocRaw`'s size argument at
// `runtime/root/root.bit:555`.
//
// No dump-based differential can see this: every `selfhost-diff*.sh`
// compares two compilers' OUTPUT over the SAME source, and both compilers
// here are self-consistent, so there is nothing to diff. The check below
// instead compares SOURCE, before any expensive or dangerous build runs:
// the declared parameter count of every `@symbol("bit_rt_x")`-exported
// `runtime/**` function THE COMPILER CAN ACTUALLY EMIT A CALL TO in the
// working tree, against the SAME symbol's arity in the git tag stage0 was
// cut from (`dist/stage0/SHA256SUMS`).
//
// NARROWED TO THE COMPILER'S OWN EMITTED-PRIMITIVE SET, #3936. The
// mechanism above only exists for a symbol `stepSelfhost`'s stage0-compiled
// `compiler/**` build ITSELF calls while compiling `compiler/**`'s OWN
// source — which happens only for the small, closed set of primitives a
// backend hardcodes a call to (`compilerEmittedSymbols()` below): the
// `Op.RtCall` roster (`compiler/irrtfns.bit`'s `rtFnTags()`) plus the dozen
// names each backend's `emitCallReloc`/`xCallReloc`/`emitCall` sites name
// directly (gc_alloc, iface_lookup, safepoint, the four panic helpers,
// world_addr/gc_addr, and the three scheduler-poll symbols). `compiler/**`
// is itself a Bit program that uses slices, maps, strings, `new`,
// interfaces and overflow-checked arithmetic throughout its OWN
// implementation, so STAGE0 — not the tree's own resulting compiler —
// emits the machine code for all of THAT usage, using stage0's own,
// possibly-stale, hardcoded arity. A symbol `compiler/**` never references
// at all (e.g. `bit_rt_port_park_wait`, `grep -rn park_wait compiler/` is
// empty) has no such call site baked into stage0's own already-compiled
// machine code: an ordinary `extern fn` call gets its arity from PARSING
// the callee's declaration out of whatever source text the compiling
// compiler is fed, never from a hardcoded primitive table like the
// Op.RtCall one above — so, unlike #3152's case, it cannot go stale.
//
// #3985: A symbol never referenced by `compiler/**` is NOT necessarily
// called ONLY from other `runtime/**` source — that was this paragraph's
// claim until #3985, and it was false for 36 of the 447 non-emitted
// symbols (re-derive: `comm -23` on every `@symbol(...)` export against
// `compilerEmittedSymbols()`'s roster, intersected with
// `grep -rhoE 'extern fn [A-Za-z0-9_]+' stdlib`). Those 36 — the crypto
// AES/GHASH/SHA256 hardware paths, the `fs`/`net` `_w` wrappers, `prof`,
// two scheduler timer helpers — ARE called from `stdlib/**`, and
// `compiler/**` imports `std/*`, so `stepSelfhost` compiles both in the
// SAME stage0 pass: those calls are compiled by stage0, not by the tree's
// own just-built compiler.
//
// What actually keeps all 36 safe: `resolveStage0()` never hands out the
// raw stage0 binary, only `scripts/stage0.sh`'s generated `bit-oracle`
// WRAPPER, which exports `BIT_STDLIB=<this tree>/stdlib` before exec'ing it
// (see that script's own "WHY THE PATH PRINTED IS A WRAPPER" comment).
// Stage0 therefore compiles the WORKING TREE's `stdlib/**` — never the
// release tarball's own bundled copy — and since an `extern fn` call's
// arity comes from that source text rather than from anything baked into
// stage0's own binary, the call it emits always matches the WORKING TREE's
// `runtime/**` that `stepLibbitrt` links in. A raw, unwrapped stage0 binary
// has no such pin: `stdRootPath()` (`compiler/mainpaths.bit`) tries
// `resolveNearExe("stdlib")` before cwd, and an unpacked stage0 release
// ships its OWN `stdlib/` beside its binary, so invoked bare it would
// compile `compiler/**`'s `std/*` imports against the RELEASE stdlib —
// reintroducing #3152's exact shape for all 36 of these symbols. The
// wrapper's `BIT_STDLIB` pin is therefore load-bearing for every
// non-emitted symbol with a `stdlib/**` caller, not incidental to it.
//
// EMPIRICALLY VERIFIED, not just reasoned (#3936): a minimal g2archive.sh
// repro mutating `bit_rt_slice_append`'s arity (an emitted primitive)
// reproducibly corrupted a linked program's output under BOTH the pinned
// stage0 and this tree's own compiler as the caller, while an analogous
// mutation to a park_wait-shaped, never-emitted symbol (declaration and
// implementation always co-compiled) was correct under all four
// compiler/runtime pairings. And the real case this guard exists for
// (#3152, `bit_rt_slice_new`+`bit_rt_slice_get`/`set`/`append`) is
// unaffected by the narrowing: all five are in `rtFnTags()`. A REAL
// single-pass build of the #3914 `bit_rt_port_park_wait` 3->4 change (at
// `3bfc9090`, guard bypassed via the documented `BIT_STAGE0_BIN` escape
// hatch) produced a compiler that ran a program and passed
// `test-golden` (571/571) — the two-pass bootstrap this guard forced for
// that change was never actually necessary.
//
// WHY THIS IS A SOUND PROXY FOR STAGE0'S OWN OPAQUE CALL-SITE TABLE, which we
// cannot read directly (it is machine code inside an already-built binary):
// a release only ships once its own selfhost build and full suite pass
// against that exact runtime, so within any single released tree, the
// compiler backend's hardcoded RtCall arity for a symbol and that symbol's
// own declared parameter count are — by construction, checked at release
// time — the same number. Comparing declared signatures across the two
// trees is therefore equivalent to comparing the two compilers' actual
// call-site behaviour, without needing to disassemble either one.
//
// Deliberately narrow: only symbols present in BOTH trees are compared. A
// brand new `@symbol(...)` export (present only in the working tree) is not
// a stage0 arity mismatch — it is an undefined symbol at link time, which
// the linker already reports on its own, with its own clear diagnostic.
//
// Keyed by (path relative to `runtime/`, symbol name), NOT by symbol name
// alone — verified necessary, not assumed: `bit_rt_port_park_mono_ns` is
// exported with 0 params from `runtime/park/darwin/wait.bit` and 1 from
// `runtime/park/linux/wait.bit` (and `_start` similarly, 3 params from
// `runtime/root/darwin/boot.bit` vs. 0 from `runtime/root/linux/boottail.bit`)
// — legitimate, unrelated per-platform entry points that happen to share a
// symbol name because only one file compiles per target. A flat name->arity
// map produced two false positives on an unmodified tree from exactly this;
// keying by file as well makes each platform's own declaration compare only
// against itself. The narrow cost: a symbol renamed or moved to a new file
// in the SAME change that also changes its arity is not caught (its old and
// new keys never coincide) — accepted, because a missed rename is a silent
// no-op here, where a false positive would block every ordinary build.
//
// Text-based, not a real parser — verified sufficient for this corpus, not
// assumed: `grep -rhA2 '@symbol(' runtime --include='*.bit' | grep '^fn ' |
// grep -v ')'` returns empty (2026-08-16), so no exported runtime function's
// signature spans more than one line, and no exported signature contains a
// generic `<...>` type argument (checked separately: no `map<` or `chan<`
// parameter type appears on any `@symbol`-decorated function).
//
// See docs/development.md, "Landing a runtime ABI change", for the two-pass
// `BIT_STAGE0_BIN` bootstrap this check exists to steer a real arity change
// toward, and for why `BIT_STAGE0_BIN` itself must skip this check rather
// than fail it.

import { trimSpace, indexOf, split, hasPrefix } from "std/strings"

class AbiSymbol {
  name: string,
  arity: int,
  file: string,
}

class AbiMismatch {
  symbol: string,
  file: string,
  treeArity: int,
  stage0Arity: int,
}

// Reads the pinned stage0's version straight out of the committed digest
// file rather than trusting its prose comment — `dist/stage0/SHA256SUMS`'s
// own tail comment says "EXACTLY ONE LINE PER TRIPLE", so any one data line
// yields the same version every other data line would.
fn stage0Version(): (string, bool) {
  let text = readFile("dist/stage0/SHA256SUMS") catch _ {
    return "", false
  }
  let suffixes = ["-linux-aarch64.tar.xz", "-linux-x86_64.tar.xz", "-macos-aarch64.tar.xz"]
  for line of split(text, "\n") {
    let trimmed = trimSpace(line)
    if (len(trimmed) == 0 || trimmed[0] == '#') {
      continue
    }
    let bitAt = indexOf(trimmed, "bit-")
    if (bitAt < 0) {
      continue
    }
    for suf of suffixes {
      if (hasSuffix(trimmed, suf)) {
        let start = bitAt + len("bit-")
        let stop = len(trimmed) - len(suf)
        if (stop > start) {
          return trimmed[start:stop], true
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return "", false
}

// Extracts `runtime/**` exactly as it existed at `tag` into a scratch
// directory, via `git archive` — local and instant when `tag` already exists
// (every release tag does, in a normal non-shallow clone of this repo), and
// refuses cleanly (non-zero, caught by the caller) rather than guessing when
// it does not.
fn extractStage0Runtime(tag: string): (string, bool) {
  let dest = "bit-out/make/abiarity-stage0-runtime"
  let script = "set -e\n" +
    "git rev-parse --verify " +
    shQuote(tag + "^{commit}") +
    " >/dev/null\n" +
    "rm -rf " +
    shQuote(dest) +
    "\n" +
    "mkdir -p " +
    shQuote(dest) +
    "\n" +
    "git archive " +
    shQuote(tag) +
    " -- runtime | tar -x -C " +
    shQuote(dest) +
    "\n"
  let rc = runShell("abiarity-stage0-runtime", script, buildTimeoutMs())
  if (rc != 0) {
    return "", false
  }
  return dest + "/runtime", true
}

// Every exported symbol under `root`. Each `AbiSymbol.file` is recorded
// relative to `root` (not the raw walked path) so the tree side
// (`parseAbiSymbols("runtime")`) and the stage0 side
// (`parseAbiSymbols("bit-out/make/.../runtime")`) produce the SAME file key
// for the same source file, which `checkRuntimeAbiArity` depends on to tell
// a real mismatch apart from an unrelated per-platform declaration sharing a
// symbol name (see this file's header).
//
// The second return is every `path: symbol` a real (non-comment) `@symbol(`
// marker was attached to but whose declared signature could not be parsed
// (#4189) — never silently dropped from the count, because a symbol this
// scan cannot see is a symbol the arity comparison below cannot protect.
fn parseAbiSymbols(root: string): ([]AbiSymbol, []string) {
  let out = []AbiSymbol(0)
  let failures = []string(0)
  let prefix = root + "/"
  for p of walkBit(root) {
    let relPath = p
    if (hasPrefix(p, prefix)) {
      relPath = p[len(prefix):]
    }
    let text = readFile(p) catch _ {
      continue
    }
    let (syms, fails) = parseAbiSymbolsInFile(relPath, text)
    for sym of syms {
      out = append(out, sym)
    }
    for f of fails {
      failures = append(failures, f)
    }
  }
  return out, failures
}

// True for a `//` comment line (leading whitespace allowed). `runtime/**`
// documents its own ABI pins in prose — `@symbol("...")` appears inside a
// comment three times as of #4189 (an example in a doc comment, and two
// cross-references to a symbol pinned elsewhere) — and a marker match inside
// one of those is not a declaration at all, so it must never reach the
// signature parser below.
fn isCommentLine(line: string): bool {
  return hasPrefix(trimSpace(line), "//")
}

// Builds the text `parseArity` needs to see a signature that wraps across
// any number of lines: `firstChunk` is already the tail of the marker's own
// line starting at the closing quote of the symbol string (never the start
// of `line` — see the comment at the call site for why), and this appends
// subsequent RAW lines onto it. `parseArity` tracks paren depth itself and
// stops at the first point that depth returns to zero, so it is safe to
// hand it more text than the real signature — including, in the common
// case, the function's own body — as long as enough lines are present to
// reach the actual closing paren. Bounded per Power-of-10: the longest
// exported signature in this corpus today is 6 params / 7 lines
// (`bit_rt_configure`, `bit_rt_bind_memory`), so 20 extra lines is a wide
// margin, not a tight fit.
fn joinSignatureWindow(lines: []string, startIdx: int, firstChunk: string): string {
  let n = len(lines)
  let bound = startIdx + 20
  if (bound > n) {
    bound = n
  }
  let joined = firstChunk
  let j = startIdx + 1
  while (j < bound) {
    joined = joined + "\n" + lines[j]
    j = j + 1
  }
  return joined
}

// Handles both shapes actually present in `runtime/**` (verified by the
// header's grep, 2026-08-16): `@symbol("x") fn name(...)` on one line, or
// `@symbol("x")` on its own line immediately above the `fn` line — and, as
// of #4189, a signature wrapped across any number of further lines, joined
// via `joinSignatureWindow` before `parseArity` ever sees it.
fn parseAbiSymbolsInFile(path: string, text: string): ([]AbiSymbol, []string) {
  let out = []AbiSymbol(0)
  let failures = []string(0)
  let lines = split(text, "\n")
  let n = len(lines)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let line = lines[i]
    if (isCommentLine(line)) {
      i = i + 1
      continue
    }
    let marker = "@symbol(\""
    let at = indexOf(line, marker)
    if (at < 0) {
      i = i + 1
      continue
    }
    let nameStart = at + len(marker)
    let rest = line[nameStart:]
    let closeAt = indexOf(rest, "\"")
    if (closeAt < 0) {
      i = i + 1
      continue
    }
    let symName = rest[:closeAt]
    // Join from the closing quote, never from the start of `line` — a
    // symbol string containing the two characters "fn" would otherwise be
    // found before the real `fn` keyword. (Checked separately: no symbol
    // name in this tree contains "fn", but the search is structured to not
    // depend on that staying true.)
    let joined = joinSignatureWindow(lines, i, rest[closeAt:])
    let (arity, ok) = parseArity(joined)
    if (ok) {
      out = append(out, AbiSymbol{ name: symName, arity: arity, file: path })
    } else {
      failures = append(failures, path + ": symbol \"" + symName + "\"")
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out, failures
}

// The index of the `)` matching the `(` at `openAt`, tracking depth so a
// nested paren (e.g. a function-typed parameter) cannot be miscounted. -1
// when `tail` never returns to depth 0 (an unterminated / unparseable
// signature). Split out of `parseArity` to keep both this and
// `countTopLevelCommas` under the complexity-10 ceiling (spec/LINT.md:186,
// E0204) after #4189 added the trailing-comma handling that function needs.
fn findMatchingParen(tail: string, openAt: int): int {
  let depth = 0
  let k = openAt
  while (k < len(tail)) {
    let c = tail[k]
    if (c == '(') {
      depth = depth + 1
    } else if (c == ')') {
      depth = depth - 1
      if (depth == 0) {
        return k
      }
    }
    k = k + 1
  }
  return -1
}

// Counts top-level (paren-depth-1) commas in a parameter list body, tracking
// depth so a nested parameter cannot be miscounted. Returns -1 for an empty
// (zero-argument) body, never 0, so the caller can tell "no parameters" apart
// from "one parameter, no commas" without a second empty check.
//
// #4189: a magic trailing comma before the closing paren -- what every
// multi-line wrapped signature in this corpus has, one param per line, e.g.
// `bit_rt_map_new`'s `capHint: int,\n): int {` -- is stripped before
// counting. Left unstripped it is indistinguishable from a real separator,
// so an N-param wrapped signature parsed as N+1: this was live and wrong the
// first time `joinSignatureWindow` made a wrapped signature reachable at
// all, reporting `bit_rt_map_new` as tree-arity 4 instead of the true 3.
fn countTopLevelCommas(rawBody: string): int {
  let body = trimSpace(rawBody)
  if (len(body) == 0) {
    return -1
  }
  if (body[len(body) - 1] == ',') {
    body = trimSpace(body[:len(body) - 1])
    if (len(body) == 0) {
      return -1
    }
  }
  let commaDepth = 0
  let commas = 0
  let m = 0
  while (m < len(body)) {
    let c = body[m]
    if (c == '(') {
      commaDepth = commaDepth + 1
    } else if (c == ')') {
      commaDepth = commaDepth - 1
    } else if (c == ',' && commaDepth == 0) {
      commas = commas + 1
    }
    m = m + 1
  }
  return commas
}

fn parseArity(fnLine: string): (int, bool) {
  let fnAt = indexOf(fnLine, "fn ")
  if (fnAt < 0) {
    return 0, false
  }
  let tail = fnLine[fnAt:]
  let openAt = indexOf(tail, "(")
  if (openAt < 0) {
    return 0, false
  }
  let bodyEnd = findMatchingParen(tail, openAt)
  if (bodyEnd < 0) {
    return 0, false
  }
  let commas = countTopLevelCommas(tail[openAt + 1:bodyEnd])
  if (commas < 0) {
    return 0, true
  }
  return commas + 1, true
}

// Composite map key: file path (relative to `runtime/`) plus symbol name,
// so a symbol name reused by two platform-specific files (see this file's
// header) never collides in `checkRuntimeAbiArity`'s comparison map.
fn abiSymbolKey(s: AbiSymbol): string {
  return s.file + ":" + s.name
}

// ---- #3936: the compiler's own emitted-primitive set ----------------------

// Extracts `compiler/irrtfns.bit`'s `rtFnTags()` string list as text, the
// same way `_tests_/bit/abimembers/scanners.bit`'s `scanRtFnTags` reads the
// identical file for the identical reason: `tools/build/**` does not import
// `compiler/**` Bit source, so the canonical roster is parsed rather than
// executed. Deliberately not a general-purpose parser — this list is one
// flat `return [ "a", "b", ... ]`, never nested, matching the file's own
// comment that it must stay a plain array for exactly this kind of reader.
fn parseRtFnTags(text: string): []string {
  // Searches for `return [`, not the first `[` after the marker: the
  // function's own `(): []string` return-type annotation contains a `[`
  // that sorts earlier than the list literal and is immediately followed
  // by `]`, which looked like a (contentless) match before this fix.
  let marker = "fn rtFnTags()"
  let at = indexOf(text, marker)
  if (at < 0) {
    return []string(0)
  }
  let retMarker = "return ["
  let openAt = indexOf(text[at:], retMarker)
  if (openAt < 0) {
    return []string(0)
  }
  let body = text[at + openAt + len(retMarker) - 1:]
  let out = []string(0)
  let i = 0
  let depth = 0
  while (i < len(body)) {
    let c = body[i]
    if (c == '[') {
      depth = depth + 1
    } else if (c == ']') {
      depth = depth - 1
      if (depth == 0) {
        break
      }
    } else if (c == '"') {
      let rest = body[i + 1:]
      let closeAt = indexOf(rest, "\"")
      if (closeAt < 0) {
        break
      }
      out = append(out, rest[:closeAt])
      i = i + 1 + closeAt
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out
}

// Mirrors `compiler/codegen.bit`'s `rtSymbol` exactly (verb-first for the
// two error-slot accessors, `bit_rt_<tag>` otherwise) — the same duplication
// `_tests_/bit/abimembers/scanners.bit`'s own local `rtSymbol` already
// carries, for the same reason: a text-based reader cannot import the
// function it mirrors.
fn rtSymbolForTag(t: string): string {
  if (t == "err_set") {
    return "bit_rt_set_err"
  }
  if (t == "err_get") {
    return "bit_rt_get_err"
  }
  return "bit_rt_${t}"
}

// The names each backend emits DIRECTLY (a literal string argument to
// `emitCallReloc`/`xCallReloc`/`emitCall`/`xEmitCallLike` in
// `compiler/arm64call.bit`, `compiler/x64call.bit`, `compiler/x64controlflow.bit`,
// `compiler/arm64select*.bit` and `compiler/x64select*.bit`) rather than
// through `Op.RtCall`'s generic `rtSymbol(tag)` path — verified by grepping
// every such call site as of #3936, excluding `compiler/*check.bit` (those
// are the compiler's own unit-test fixtures, not real emission).
//
// `bit_rt_port_gc_thread_token` and `bit_rt_safepoint_needed` are
// DELIBERATELY absent though each has a `*Symbol()` helper here too:
// `compiler/codegen.bit`'s own comment on `gcThreadTokenSymbol` says the
// backends inline that primitive's body instead of ever emitting a `bl` to
// it (confirmed: `arm64call.bit`'s `emitCall` intercepts the symbol name and
// calls `emitInlinedThreadToken` instead of `emitCallReloc`), and no backend
// emits `bit_rt_safepoint_needed` any more (#2925 arm64, #3169 x64). Neither
// is a real call site, so neither can develop this ABI hazard — same shape
// as `bit_rt_port_park_wait`, just inside `compiler/**` instead of outside it.
fn directEmissionSymbols(): []string {
  return [
    "bit_rt_gc_alloc", "bit_rt_iface_lookup", "bit_rt_safepoint",
    "bit_rt_panic_div_zero", "bit_rt_panic_overflow",
    "bit_rt_panic_nil_call", "bit_rt_panic_nil_iface",
    "bit_rt_world_addr", "bit_rt_gc_addr",
    "bit_rt_port_sched_preempt_any_addr",
    "bit_rt_port_sched_current_worker",
    "bit_rt_port_sched_preempt_requested",
  ]
}

// The full set of runtime symbol names this tree's `compiler/**` can
// actually emit a call to (#3936) — see this file's header for why only
// these, not every `@symbol(...)` export, are at risk from a stage0/tree
// ABI pairing. `ok` is false when `compiler/irrtfns.bit` could not be read
// or its roster parsed with reasonable confidence; the caller then falls
// back to checking every exported symbol (the pre-#3936 behaviour) rather
// than silently narrowing on data it could not verify.
fn compilerEmittedSymbols(): (map<string, bool>, bool) {
  let text = readFile("compiler/irrtfns.bit") catch _ {
    return map<string, bool>{}, false
  }
  let tags = parseRtFnTags(text)
  // Mirrors abimembers.bit's own `minRtFnTags` floor (90 against ~96 today):
  // a short parse is a fact about this reader, not about the roster.
  if (len(tags) < 90) {
    return map<string, bool>{}, false
  }
  let out = map<string, bool>{}
  for t of tags {
    out[rtSymbolForTag(t)] = true
  }
  for s of directEmissionSymbols() {
    out[s] = true
  }
  return out, true
}

// Compares `treeSymbols`' declared arity against `stage0Arity` and returns
// every real mismatch — split out of `checkRuntimeAbiArity` to keep both
// under the complexity-10 ceiling (spec/LINT.md:186, E0204).
//
// #3936: only a symbol the compiler itself can emit a call to is at risk
// from a stage0-baked-arity mismatch (see this file's header for the two
// ways a non-emitted symbol still avoids it — a `runtime/**`-only caller
// compiled fresh by the tree's own compiler, or a `stdlib/**` caller
// compiled by stage0 under the `bit-oracle` wrapper's `BIT_STDLIB` pin).
// `compilerEmittedSymbols`'s `ok` false means the emitted-set parse failed,
// so every mismatch is kept (the pre-#3936, more conservative behaviour)
// rather than silently narrowing on data this function could not verify.
fn collectArityMismatches(treeSymbols: []AbiSymbol, stage0Arity: map<string, int>): []AbiMismatch {
  let (emitted, emittedOk) = compilerEmittedSymbols()
  let mismatches = []AbiMismatch(0)
  for s of treeSymbols {
    let (oldArity, ok) = stage0Arity[abiSymbolKey(s)]
    if (!ok || oldArity == s.arity) {
      continue
    }
    if (emittedOk && !emitted[s.name]) {
      continue
    }
    mismatches = append(
      mismatches,
      AbiMismatch{ symbol: s.name, file: s.file, treeArity: s.arity, stage0Arity: oldArity },
    )
  }
  return mismatches
}

// The outcome of scanning one side (working tree or extracted stage0 tag)
// for exported symbols. `stop == true` means the caller must return
// immediately with `buildOk` as its own return value; `stop == false` means
// `symbols` is a complete, trustworthy table safe to compare.
class AbiScanResult {
  symbols: []AbiSymbol,
  stop: bool,
  buildOk: bool,
}

// A symbol whose `@symbol(...)` marker was found attached to real
// (non-comment) source but whose declared signature `parseArity` still could
// not parse, even after `joinSignatureWindow` joined the surrounding lines.
// This — not a low total count — was the actual defect (#4189): a bare
// `if (ok) { append }` with no `else` silently dropped exactly this case, so
// the arity comparison below never saw 164 of 783 real declarations,
// `bit_rt_map_new` and `bit_rt_slice_new` among them. A guard that drops what
// it cannot read is not a guard, so this refuses the build outright rather
// than comparing a partial table.
fn reportAbiParseFailures(where: string, failures: []string): bool {
  eprint("make: selfhost: refusing to link — could not parse the declared signature for ${len(failures)} exported symbol(s) in ${where}:\n")
  for f of failures {
    eprint("make:   ${f}\n")
  }
  eprint("make: the runtime ABI arity check cannot trust a partial symbol table — fix the signature's formatting (or this parser, tools/build/abiarity.bit) rather than let it silently drop the symbol.\n")
  return false
}

// Parses every exported symbol under `root` and decides whether the result
// is safe to use: any individual parse failure stops the build outright
// (`reportAbiParseFailures`); a suspiciously small total (below `minParsed`)
// is instead treated as an infrastructure fact about this reader — same
// posture `compilerEmittedSymbols` already takes at its own floor of 90 —
// and skips just this check rather than blocking an unrelated build.
fn scanAbiSide(root: string, label: string, minParsed: int): AbiScanResult {
  let (symbols, failures) = parseAbiSymbols(root)
  if (len(failures) > 0) {
    let buildOk = reportAbiParseFailures(label, failures)
    return AbiScanResult{ symbols: symbols, stop: true, buildOk: buildOk }
  }
  if (len(symbols) < minParsed) {
    eprint("make: selfhost: parsed only ${len(symbols)} exported symbol(s) from ${label} (want >= ${minParsed}) — too few to trust; skipping the runtime ABI arity check\n")
    return AbiScanResult{ symbols: symbols, stop: true, buildOk: true }
  }
  return AbiScanResult{ symbols: symbols, stop: false, buildOk: true }
}

// The entry point `stepSelfhost` calls before linking. Returns false (and
// has already printed the diagnostic) when the build must not proceed.
//
// Skips itself under `BIT_STAGE0_BIN`: that override is not the pinned
// release, so there is no committed tag to diff its tree against — and it is
// the DOCUMENTED two-pass path for landing an arity change (see
// docs/development.md), not a mismatch to refuse. Skips itself (with a
// warning, not a failure) when the pinned version or its tag cannot be
// resolved, so an infrastructure hiccup unrelated to this bug class does not
// turn into a new, unrelated build failure.
fn checkRuntimeAbiArity(): bool {
  if (env("BIT_STAGE0_BIN") != "") {
    return true
  }
  let (version, vok) = stage0Version()
  if (!vok) {
    eprint("make: selfhost: cannot resolve pinned stage0 version from dist/stage0/SHA256SUMS; skipping the runtime ABI arity check\n")
    return true
  }
  let tag = "v${version}"
  let (stage0RuntimeDir, dok) = extractStage0Runtime(tag)
  if (!dok) {
    eprint("make: selfhost: cannot extract runtime/** at ${tag} (tag missing locally, or `git archive` failed); skipping the runtime ABI arity check\n")
    return true
  }
  // 780: the working tree parses to exactly this many real (non-comment)
  // exported declarations as of #4189, with zero drops — see this file's
  // header for the corpus grep. A floor here, not just a print, mirrors
  // `compilerEmittedSymbols`'s own floor of 90 against its ~96.
  let treeScan = scanAbiSide("runtime", "runtime/**", 780)
  if (treeScan.stop) {
    return treeScan.buildOk
  }
  eprint("make: selfhost: runtime ABI arity scan: parsed=${len(treeScan.symbols)} dropped=0\n")
  let stage0Scan = scanAbiSide(stage0RuntimeDir, "${tag}'s runtime/**", 10)
  if (stage0Scan.stop) {
    return stage0Scan.buildOk
  }
  let treeSymbols = treeScan.symbols
  let stage0Symbols = stage0Scan.symbols
  let stage0Arity = map<string, int>{}
  for s of stage0Symbols {
    stage0Arity[abiSymbolKey(s)] = s.arity
  }
  let mismatches = collectArityMismatches(treeSymbols, stage0Arity)
  if (len(mismatches) > 0) {
    eprint("make: selfhost: refusing to link — runtime ABI arity mismatch against the pinned stage0 (${tag}):\n")
    for m of mismatches {
      eprint("make:   ${m.symbol} (${m.file}): stage0 (${tag}) emits calls for ${m.stage0Arity} arg(s); this tree's runtime/** now declares ${m.treeArity}\n")
    }
    eprint("make: stage0's call-site lowering for these symbols is baked into its own compiled machine code and does not re-read runtime/** — linking would succeed and the callee would read an unwritten argument register (#3152).\n")
    eprint("make: land this with the two-pass BIT_STAGE0_BIN bootstrap instead (docs/development.md, \"Landing a runtime ABI change\"):\n")
    eprint("make:   rm -rf bit-out\n")
    eprint("make:   git checkout <commit before this runtime/** change> -- runtime/  # pass 1 needs the OLD runtime, matching stage0's baked-in arity\n")
    eprint("make:   ./make                                                          # pass 1: stage0 -> a compiler from the new compiler/** source, linked against the OLD runtime\n")
    eprint("make:   cp bit-out/bin/bit $TMPDIR/bit1\n")
    eprint("make:   git checkout HEAD -- runtime/                                   # restore BEFORE anything else reads the tree\n")
    eprint("make:   BIT_STAGE0_BIN=$TMPDIR/bit1 ./make                              # pass 2 -- do NOT rm -rf bit-out here\n")
    return false
  }
  // Arities match; the same extracted stage0RuntimeDir also answers the
  // narrower, LAYOUT question this check was blind to (#3891, #3893).
  return checkRuntimeAbiLayout(stage0RuntimeDir, tag)
}

// #4197: read-only utility for `dist/abitwopass.py`'s git archaeology during
// a runtime ABI transition (the two-pass bootstrap this file's refusal
// message above points at). Prints every `@symbol(...)`-exported
// declaration under $BIT_ABI_SCAN_DIR as `name\tarity\tfile` — reusing
// `parseAbiSymbols`, the SAME parser `checkRuntimeAbiArity`'s own mismatch
// detection trusts, rather than a second, hand-rolled reader that could
// silently drift from it. This lets a caller outside this driver ask "what
// arity did symbol X have in this historical runtime/ snapshot" without
// re-deriving the parsing rules. Informational only: never gated, never
// part of `test`.
fn stepDumpAbiSymbols(): int {
  let dir = env("BIT_ABI_SCAN_DIR")
  if (dir == "") {
    eprint("make: dump-abi-symbols: BIT_ABI_SCAN_DIR is not set\n")
    return 1
  }
  let (symbols, failures) = parseAbiSymbols(dir)
  if (len(failures) > 0) {
    eprint("make: dump-abi-symbols: could not parse the declared signature for ${len(failures)} exported symbol(s) in ${dir}:\n")
    for f of failures {
      eprint("make:   ${f}\n")
    }
    return 1
  }
  for s of symbols {
    print("${s.name}\t${s.arity}\t${s.file}\n")
  }
  return 0
}
